WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.280
All right, hi everybody and welcome back
to the channel.

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:05.789
My name is Bradley Knapp, and I'm one of the Product Managers here at IBM Cloud,

00:00:05.789 --> 00:00:07.920
and what I want to talk with you guys about today

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.900
is a question that we get fairly commonly

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.600
when folks are starting their cloud journey

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:12.960
and starting to learn about cloud,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.460
and that's, "What is IaaS?"

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.320
I read about cloud, I see this IaaS thing everywhere,

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.040
what does it actually mean?

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.369
And, so, "IaaS" is an acronym, and so it's broken into 2 parts:

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:26.360
the first part, the "I", that's "infrastructure".

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.040
And, so, if you think of cloud

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.480
as being just some other dude's computer,

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.931
running somewhere else,

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.840
that's the infrastructure part.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.880
And, so, that infrastructure, if it's not cloud -

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:39.180
it could be running in a data center
somewhere,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.260
it can be running in a closet somewhere,

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.220
your laptop or your desktop is infrastructure.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.980
And then the "a.a.S." piece is "as-a-Service".

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.580
That's the billing method, that's the way that you consume it.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.160
And there are other kinds of as a
service.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.600
You've got "PaaS", Platform as a Service,

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.780
you have "SaaS", Software as a
Service.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:03.060
There's lots of different kinds of things that you can consume as-a-Service

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.600
but very specifically what we want to talk about

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.940
is the "I", it's the infrastructure.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.820
And so I've got this diagram written out over here

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.770
because infrastructure really falls into three

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.600
main categories, right.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.659
The first category is going to be compute, that's where the

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:23.400
processors are that's where the actual
lifting and computing gets done. The

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.570
second piece which is storage kind of
falls into three main buckets and lots

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.840
of smaller buckets on top of it because
there's different kinds of storage. And

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.110
then the third piece, the piece that ties
everything together

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:38.939
that's our network piece. And so we're
gonna draw this one over here because

00:01:38.939 --> 00:01:42.720
without network you can't do anything, network is how the compute talks to the

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.939
storage and that's how the compute talks
to the other compute. And so like I said

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:51.630
we can break this down into different
pieces and so in the

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:55.200
compute side I've got three things
called out up here, the first one is kind

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.189
of I've just got a labeled compute, its
general purpose compute, right. This is

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:03.689
your normal web server or application
server, it can really be whatever general

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:08.819
purpose kind of computing needs you have.
The second two are, or the second and the

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:12.550
third really are more specific right. So
GPU is

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:17.440
is a graphics processor, that's a very
very high speed processor that's used in

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:21.910
conjunction with a traditional processor
for specific kinds of workloads right.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:26.410
This is gonna be your machine learning
and your AI. And then the third piece, HPC,

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:30.220
that's high-performance computing. So
there's specific kinds of workloads that

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:34.990
had very specific requirements as far as
frequency which is your clock speed and

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:38.380
the number of cores that are required
where you have to have lots of power

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.940
packed into a very, very, very small
footprint, that's gonna be your HPC. And

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:46.090
likewise on the storage side you've got
different kinds of storage because you

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:50.350
have different storage needs. The most
commonly used one is gonna be object

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:55.420
storage. Object storage is a little bit
lower performance but it's relatively

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:59.620
inexpensive and that's for your general
purpose storage right. What goes into

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:05.320
object storage? Well you can have things
like pictures, you can have documents, you

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.400
can have really whatever you want can go
into that object storage, it's where all

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:14.530
of the data and all of the graphics on
the web server that's all hiding

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:19.150
in object storage. And then the second
and the third piece that I've got called

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:24.010
out here, block and file, these are
specific kinds of storage, specific kinds

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:28.000
of network storage, and they attach in
very specific ways. Block storage

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.770
attaches with iSCSI, file storage
attaches with NFS, it's the way that they

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:37.840
mount into the actual compute itself. And
there are specific kinds of applications

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.260
that require block storage or file
storage because each of them has their

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:46.240
own features and benefits. And so to talk
about how we pull all of these things

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:52.150
together we need to talk about the
network, because network has two main

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.840
components that matter. And so what I
want you to do is I want you to think of

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.640
your network as a pipe, right. And so a
network can be a small pipe, that would

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.230
be like a pipe measured in megabits so
you can't press much data through it. Or

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:08.950
it can be a very large pipe, that very
large pipe that would be measured in

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:14.350
gigabits per second. And so the more data
you need to push simultaneously the

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:18.310
larger pipe you need and the more
bandwidth you need. The second way that

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:23.350
we measure network traffic is how much
data gets pushed through this pipe over

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.410
a set period of time. Normally it's
billed by the

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.670
month but it could also be billed by the
minute, by the second, or maybe even by

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:34.690
the day, or by the week. And so to take
all of this and tie all this together, I

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:39.790
want to use an example of something that
requires some specialty components right,

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:43.660
we're going to talk a little bit about
an AI workload. And so if you think about

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:48.700
an AI workload where you're going to do
automatic visual recognition of pictures.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.510
Let's say that you have a billion
pictures down here in object storage

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:57.700
that you are then going to use to train
your model that's running on these GPU

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:01.810
servers, and so you take that billion
pictures and since a billion is a lot

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:05.830
and pictures are very large you have to
push them through a really big pipe, that's

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:11.980
your network pipe up into the GPU server
but the GPU server doesn't have any

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:15.940
storage inherent to it. So that GPU
server is actually going to take and

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:21.430
write that into block. And it's going to
write that data back and forth, and back

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:26.470
and forth until the model is done. Once
it's trained it's going to take all of

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:30.130
the data that we pushed up here and all
of the results and it's going to write

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:34.600
all of that back down into object
storage. Why object storage? Because

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.160
again, it's less expensive, it's a good
archiving solution. And so you're pushing

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:43.090
a ton of data through these pipes while
they're turned on and then once you're

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:48.820
done you get rid of them. And so the
second piece that I want to talk about

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:54.070
is the "as-a-Service" piece, this is the
way that you consume. And so when we talk

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:57.790
about as-a-Service there are kind of
four things that really, really matter in

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:03.040
this model and the first one is that
offerings that are consumed as-a-Service

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:08.080
are generally speaking shared. And so by
shared I mean they're multi-tenant, many

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.010
people use the same offering, we just
take and carve it up and make it

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:15.669
available to multiple different
customers simultaneously. So that's the

00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:21.070
first piece of as-a-Service. The second
piece is the hourly or monthly piece.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:26.410
This is talking about how we bill. 
In the case of compute, it could be a

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:30.580
certain number of cents, or certain of
dollars per hour, or per month. In the

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:34.510
case of storage, we would bill out in the
amount of data that's stored in a given

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:38.530
month, so that would be cents per
gigabyte per month. In the case of

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.210
network, there are two different metrics
we

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:44.800
about earlier right. The size of the pipe
you would pay per month charge for that,

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.639
and then the amount of data that goes
through it again measured in gigabytes

00:06:48.639 --> 00:06:53.259
per month, or cents per gigabytes per
month. So that's our billing metric. And

00:06:53.259 --> 00:06:56.530
then the third piece, and this is a very
important one, is that there are no

00:06:56.530 --> 00:07:01.229
contracts involved in an as-a-Service
model, or there aren't necessarily

00:07:01.229 --> 00:07:05.410
contracts. There can certainly be them
but they're generally advantageous to

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:10.960
you. By no contracts we mean that you
don't have to agree to use something for

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:14.949
a set amount of time, you use it for as
long as you need it and then you get rid

00:07:14.949 --> 00:07:18.400
of it. And so rather than a checkmark for
no contracts I'm just gonna put a little

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:23.650
X there. You only use it to when you need
it, it's on demand. And then the last

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:28.150
piece, and this is probably the most
important as-a-Service offerings are

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:32.620
self service. That means that you can go
out to a website, you punch in your

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:37.539
information, your payment details, click
the Go button and that as-a-Service

00:07:37.539 --> 00:07:41.530
offering is going to be provisioned and
delivered to you. It's not something that

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:45.520
takes days or weeks or months to set up
and configure, it's one that can be

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:49.360
provided in minutes or hours. Thanks for
stopping by today. If you have any

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:53.259
questions, please drop us a line below in
the comments. If you want to see more

00:07:53.259 --> 00:07:57.130
videos like this in the future, please do
hit that like and subscribe button that

00:07:57.130 --> 00:08:00.789
way we know you're interested in it. And
don't forget you can always get started

00:08:00.789 --> 00:08:07.800
and cloud at no cost by signing up for a
free IBM Cloud account at cloud.ibm.com.

